#!/bin/bash
# Real bug found via repeated real full install + reboot tests on
# actual UEFI hardware, reproduced in a VM (2026-09-23): even after
# fixing the ESP population (Release 12), the /boot btrfs-subvolume
# layout (Release 13), and making @root the filesystem's default
# subvolume (Release 14), GRUB's own generated grub.cfg still produced
# a boot menu with no real OS entry -- only the auto-generated "UEFI
# Firmware Settings" fallback. Confirmed directly, twice: pressing 'c'
# for GRUB's own command line immediately after selecting the
# (correct, verified) NVRAM boot entry showed only that fallback
# entry at the GRUB menu itself, and a hand-written static menuentry
# added via custom.cfg (which this profile's grub.cfg does source,
# per its own stock /etc/grub.d/41_custom section) still didn't
# resolve it.
#
# Root cause: this profile's target ends up using GRUB's BLS
# (Boot Loader Specification) support -- grub.cfg's `blscfg` command
# dynamically reads /boot/loader/entries/*.conf at real boot time
# instead of embedding a real, static menuentry in grub.cfg itself.
# GRUB's own built-in btrfs filesystem driver is a from-scratch
# reimplementation of btrfs (not the Linux kernel's own btrfs/VFS
# logic) and evidently does not resolve this profile's named-
# subvolume (@root) layout the same way blscfg needs to find those
# loader entries, regardless of the default-subvolume fix that
# correctly fixed a real kernel mount's view of the same raw
# partition.
#
# Fixed by not using BLS at all: appends GRUB_ENABLE_BLSCFG=false to
# /etc/default/grub (Calamares' own `grubcfg` module already wrote
# this file without that setting -- this must run AFTER that module
# and BEFORE `bootloader`, whose install_secureboot() calls
# grub2-mkconfig, to actually take effect). With BLS disabled,
# grub2-mkconfig falls back to its older, far more battle-tested
# /etc/grub.d/10_linux code path, which generates a real, static,
# embedded `menuentry` block directly in grub.cfg with the correct
# subvolume-aware linux/initrd paths -- confirmed directly: this
# produces a real, syntactically correct classic menuentry (verified
# by hand against the real installed disk) where BLS's dynamic
# blscfg-based menu produced nothing.
set -e

echo "GRUB_ENABLE_BLSCFG=false" >> /etc/default/grub
